    "title": "九上阅读 · 二十五 广东许多学生具备说粤语和普通话的",
    "passage": "Many students in Guangdong find learning English difficult, but they often overlook a special skill they already have: being bilingual (双语) in Cantonese and Putonghua.\n\nZhang Tingting, a 14-year-old from Guangzhou, is a perfect example. “I’ve spoken Cantonese at home ever since I was little, but I use Putonghua at school,” she explained. “We are now encouraged to use Putonghua in classrooms, and it’s natural for students to speak Putonghua even during class breaks.”\n\nThis is common for many local students. However, with more students from other parts of China moving to Guangzhou, the use of Cantonese is declining. A report shows that now only about 40 percent of students in Guangzhou can fully understand their local dialect (方言). As a result, Putonghua is often heard even during school breaks.\n\nThis situation has raised concerns. Many people are worried that the local culture is dying as the dialect is no longer popular. “The most vivid words and expressions are born of dialects,” noted a language expert. Zhang Tingting fully agrees with his idea. She sometimes finds it strange and difficult to express certain Cantonese phrases in Putonghua, feeling that something of the local culture may be lost.\n\nFortunately, officials in Guangdong have found this problem.  ▲  As we all know, some primary schools in Shanghai have asked students to speak the Shanghai dialect four days a month. Inspired by Shanghai, starting from this year, there are almost 30 Guangzhou kindergartens encouraging children to speak their local dialect during breaks.\n\nOf course, promoting Cantonese doesn’t mean rejecting Putonghua. Instead, it helps create a multicultural environment where local expressions make our communication richer and more lively.",
    "passageHtml": "<p><span class=\"ind\">Many students in Guangdong find learning English difficult, but they often overlook a special skill they already have: being bilingual (双语) in Cantonese and Putonghua.</span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">Zhang Tingting, a 14-year-old from Guangzhou, is a perfect example. “I’ve spoken Cantonese at home ever since I was little, but I use Putonghua at school,” she explained. “We are now encouraged to use Putonghua in classrooms, and it’s natural for students to speak Putonghua even during class breaks.”</span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">This is common for many local students. However, with more students from other parts of China moving to Guangzhou, the use of Cantonese is declining. A report shows that now only about 40 percent of students in Guangzhou can fully understand their local dialect (方言). As a result, Putonghua is often heard even during school breaks.</span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">This situation has raised concerns. Many people are worried that the local culture is dying as the dialect is no longer popular. “The most <u>vivid</u> words and expressions are born of dialects,” noted a language expert. Zhang Tingting fully agrees with his idea. She sometimes finds it strange and difficult to express certain Cantonese phrases in Putonghua, feeling that something of the local culture may be lost.</span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">Fortunately, officials in Guangdong have found this problem. <u> ▲ </u> As we all know, some primary schools in Shanghai have asked students to speak the Shanghai dialect four days a month. Inspired by Shanghai, starting from this year, there are almost 30 Guangzhou kindergartens encouraging children to speak their local dialect during breaks.</span></p><p><span class=\"ind\">Of course, promoting Cantonese doesn’t mean rejecting Putonghua. Instead, it helps create a multicultural environment where local expressions make our communication richer and more lively.</span></p>",
